prompt2_q8_dependency_rebind_prohibition: "Do not modify, broaden, or replace remote-exact Prompt2 implementation `fb321d4ce9eec0510162a27e4527bc626a307755` without a new output-free incompatible registration and complete descendant invalidation. Do not accept old profile `b317ae0207522399e63125c57daf91d97432f95b26bd8fb71db746934ff05c8c`, old artifact `d3a123f327e7c4c3eb53d642c4b8d98c39a0b30bac2cc1970922612ae502f142`, or either superseded SLM2 digest except as hostile input; rerun accepted Tokenizer2, Template2, or Sampling2 evidence; alter rendering, tokenization, recomposition, controls, stops, seed, wires, limits, or failure semantics; add negotiation, alias, migration, fallback, repair, runtime repacking, dense-F32 execution, retry, best-of-N, or weaker tests; or call format/fixture evidence a model, quality, browser chat, release, deployment, P2P, MemoryEndpoints, or campaign pass."
acg2_champion_format_prohibition: "Do not create ACG2 output from terminal registration `39feda199eea782ff32f9ffb2b93f7c1a1b21d33`, edit it into a pass, or accept its stale Prompt2 identity. Only remote-exact corrected registration `416c3c1154b86feed5bdc41751609f4670462981` authorizes implementation. Do not reinterpret its `.v2` filename, `technicalRevision: 2`, ACG2 name, schema identity, or branch as a product V2 or compatibility generation. Do not weaken Prompt2 dependency closure; add ACG1, migration, negotiation, alias, optional member, adapter, overlay, router record, embedded bytes, mutable locator, partial activation, hidden retry, fallback, or second executable path; mutate active state before complete validation; or call preregistration/fixture evidence model, quality, browser chat, release, deployment, publication, P2P, MemoryEndpoints, or campaign evidence."
slm2_q8_weight_profile_regression_prohibition: "Do not reinterpret SLM2 or any numbered storage, schema, hash-domain, semantic-version, or branch identity as a TinyRustLM V2 product. Do not replace remote-exact mixed-Q8 implementation `005be4efbcacd0301888df0b12d0db9f1611be58` with a generic `Q8` or `q8_0` alias, dense-F32 production model path, second model format, compatibility reader/writer, migration, negotiation, runtime repacking, inferred dtype, permissive tail, unknown-profile fallback, TurboQuant/TurboVec attribution, or one-bit claim. Do not restore the deleted dense-F32 executable vectors, generator, tests, or fuzz profile. Preserve exact role-bound row scales and signed codes `[-127,127]`, F32 normalization and optional biases, independent F32 KV, direct validated-byte execution without an F32 weight shadow, independent Node/Rust oracles, exact hostile verdicts, and Node/real-Chromium WASM conformance. Fixture evidence must not be called real-model parity, quality, package, deployment, publication, P2P, MemoryEndpoints, or campaign progress."
bounded_release_store_regression_prohibition: "Do not reintroduce a full receipt/subject store rebuild inside any item loop, scan membership from disk for same-open snapshots, split one bounded suffix into per-item durable publisher calls, accept a non-prefix resume state, write the final publication receipt before every claim is durable, weaken exact target/durability byte validation, remove restart boundaries, reduce the 0-through-1,024 prefix corpus, increase the 180,000 ms ceiling, or call timeout growth an optimization. Preserve the current one-open rebuild, immutable snapshot, shared receipt-closure, bounded `putMany`, exact-prefix, physical-restart, and commit-last semantics. Local performance evidence is not a model, quality, release, deployment, publication, MemoryEndpoints, P2P, or campaign pass."
historical_clean_gate_performance_prohibition: "Do not normalize or hide that complete generated-byte publication once took 6,381,891.9148 ms and shard 8 took 7,853,326.4993 ms on clean source `a84125ff591f4029cdfa31c0a1e2a7ac0bb6e5b7`. Preserve old run `ab6aaee4e6c22822877ca629556dffee700f9dda05970d51e9f1761d9a5a4521` and aggregate `31e5e8ed29337d3a053b2875c32a05c03d2c8e8f7f9e195449bb3d4c591c314f`, the 151,129.3815 ms pre-generation-only development pass, the 168,988.6254 ms dirty-tree corrected pass, the first unscored wrapper-output-loss invocation, and all failed development attempts separately. The corrected clean source is exactly commit `5c632d6cb101595c675e4c404be082087b8f2f13`; run `4ec348ab4d790739add83c01ed5775e34626ddae0bb5c86c9b6cc2231fa82409` passes 10/10 shards and 23/23 tests, the target passes in 165,509.3389 ms, and maintained plus independent verification reproduce 3,995-byte aggregate `a2cca011202ebcf0b82b962aeccf012a84051189e95f8679c6f9a7f6463df91d`. Do not broaden that local algorithm gate into model, quality, package, deployment, publication, P2P, MemoryEndpoints, or campaign evidence."
unpublished_install_smoke_prohibition: "Do not restore browser-smoke logic that requires an unpublished package link or install command, and do not hide the failed 1,052/1,054 source run. While publication is blocked, `/companion/` must expose exact pending and deployment-blocked state and the real-browser smoke must fail if a UAIX NuGet package link or `dotnet add package`/`dotnet tool install` command appears. Preserve failure-record SHA256 `12134eee50c7a2589e90b6f7d41260434f68524b77dec1d62d3c225794e21b50`; rerun only from a new clean commit and fresh root."
scope: public_site_voice_project_copy_and_local_app_consent
purpose: Keep TinyRustLM browser-first, preserve constructive local-model craft and user agency, and prohibit unexpected attempts to access programs on a user's computer.
prelaunch_single_product_prohibition: "Do not describe, market, document, or implement TinyRustLM as an old product plus V2 while prelaunch. No user population or public contract exists that can justify legacy support, migration machinery, compatibility cost, or technical debt. Do not add or retain a versioned companion route family, protocol negotiation, compatibility header, alias, migration reader, old-state reader, old-preference reader, deprecated executable path, dual contract, fallback, dead branch, knowingly stale fixture, duplicated owner, temporary architecture, low-quality patch, weakened test, deferred cleanup, or documentation that presents technical revision names as product generations. Technical identities such as SLM2, Tokenizer2, Template2, Sampling2, Prompt2, ACG2, semantic package versions, schema versions, paths, and source-control branch names identify exact bytes or revisions only. Replace all dogfood producers and consumers together, verify the replacement end to end, and delete superseded executable behavior in the same completed major change. Rewrite coherently when that yields the strongest design. Immutable evidence history is audit truth, not support authority."
canonical_companion_claim_prohibition: "Do not restore `/v0`, `/v1`, `/v2`, `X-TinyRustLM-Bridge-Version`, protocol-version response fields, old cross-tab/state/preference identities, the old `TinyRustLM bridge conversion v2` operation-ID domain, the `[REDACTED]` public-identifier reader, or the superseded acquisition test filename. Do not expose a dead NuGet link or install command for unpublished `0.3.0`. Do not call local source tests, a local package, Chromium UI verification, or deep acquisition verification package publication, site deployment, public readback, real-model quality, Prompt2 full-artifact proof, outside P2P reachability, MemoryEndpoints operation, or campaign progress. Preserve failed missing-binary report SHA256 `c01876f29db3730ea4d33d3386c5f4c9d9a9a526691c283c3d19a8fc2564e7e3`, old-path pass SHA256 `efa5d40858812b1a8e19b5e1517800a18cb65a4de5bea4e6b9dfc43d902d862d`, and the canonical-SLM2 50/53 diagnostic without conflation. Never call a 53/53 run over 8,832-byte SLM1 fixture `6af985853325eaa6d3eaaa8aadd3511da9596eb542fb2d03a9bc9dc1f4377adf` SLM2 evidence; the 8,448-byte SLM2 artifact `c010e2818c64713310699e9ad928d3b22cfeb004756ae8e03acaaee5897e613a` exposes three active obsolete assumptions."
companion_publication_prohibition: "Do not deploy web assets that instruct users to install SeedHost `0.3.0` until the exact library, tool, and symbol packages pass clean-consumer verification, are published to the authorized feed, and are independently read back by immutable identity. Package source version, local package creation, a local feed, or a NuGet URL does not prove publication."
slm1_executable_prohibition: "Do not publish package `0.3.0`, deploy the product, or claim a clean SLM2-only architecture while `tinyrustlm/runtime`, `tinyrustlm/tools/slm_pack`, related workspace crates, current docs, tests, fixtures, manifests, filename rules, or release scripts still execute, author, or assume SLM1. Do not rename SLM1, wrap it behind an alias or adapter, preserve it as a temporary fallback, or weaken SLM2 tests to make it pass. First preregister the complete SLM2 browser/package/converter consumer closure and explicit frozen-root-Cargo invalidation, then update every active consumer and delete SLM1 executable source, workspace membership, current guidance, tests, fixtures, manifests, and release inputs in one verified replacement. Historical research and immutable evidence may retain SLM1 text only as non-executable history."
prompt2_claim_and_mutation_prohibition: "Preserve old Prompt2 profile `b317ae0207522399e63125c57daf91d97432f95b26bd8fb71db746934ff05c8c`, vectors `9d3354785614ff60edc679b6fea6797164ffac368edaa9d3d50912b525048cc0`, artifact `d3a123f327e7c4c3eb53d642c4b8d98c39a0b30bac2cc1970922612ae502f142`, and Node source `e993bde73b69422197e50b968869f19cfd9093cfc3aec4472205a67d47efdedd` only as the immutable pre-rebind snapshot. Do not accept those bytes in current execution, silently mutate them without registered descendant invalidation, or retain them through a compatibility composer. Preserve standalone lock `48dca83e7e45403cc012835e593db612003ed49c5fd9024473f1f52a9af21b29`; do not repair, truncate, normalize, reorder, retry, select, double-insert controls, weaken strict JSON, or infer product claims from local vectors."
prompt2_execution_preregistration_prohibition: "Do not supply the full accepted Tokenizer2 artifact to Prompt2 for comparative native or browser output before an output-free corpus, exact native/WASM interfaces, adapters, worker runner, browser identities, profile schedule, process closure, profile deletion, zero-network rule, cancellation/recovery, resource ceilings, create-only output roots, verifier, and source-control parent are frozen, committed, pushed, fetched, and actual-remote exact. Do not reuse prior Tokenizer2 or Template2 outputs as a new run, invoke any producer or verifier more than registered, overwrite terminal evidence, or convert failure into retry, skip, pass, release, or campaign credit."
clean_source_gate_truth_prohibition: "Preserve the dirty 2026-08-23 Prompt2 wrapper attempt as incomplete: its standard partition passed 1,053 with zero failures and one truthful skip across 1,054, but release gating terminated with `release_gate_source_not_clean` and produced no release receipt. Preserve interrupted clean run `9a5d66e1122bcfe4a2c1be65931bc2c85cc16ff32ba98f7a4f638a46137f9d56` with five shard receipts and no aggregate. Do not conflate either history with clean run `51b92c7506327fdd3172d334597c351f94f0eeeea6e244d0eeb65f5a3b5902cc`, whose 23/23 local tests and 3,995-byte aggregate SHA256 `93a123724c822db7f93b4f938d91d4e27949c146e4327517f391a671b2b854b9` pass maintained verification. That aggregate is local repository evidence only, never product release, deployment, publication, model, browser, or campaign evidence."
sampling2_claim_and_mutation_prohibition: "Preserve clean-slate Sampling2 commit `78aeb8d0f95f95330f97a572c5ef02b8d2b41fd5`, profile `ec65e8f1a54c1e4ca95ebda88bde091dad11337250e541bbda60e8cac311428d`, normative table `fce2345bd4ddc53f137ecc36a48afca02a73ebd3549acce942628e440429c677`, vectors `4fe731f2e8714e9a6fe7689964a88a7fbe8150faf6af053cbf0be38390daaa9f`, and artifact `9e02fcfbba169330a2ee1bf40cea015dc4067925e051f6ae5f197a5f880bfe89`. Do not edit or regenerate accepted bytes silently; substitute host exp, floating-point ordering, modulo-biased selection, a different PRNG, implicit defaults, unordered stop sets, prompt-history repetition, automatic stop append, output repair, retries, best-of-N, fallback sampling, aliases, version ranges, migrations, legacy readers, or compatibility shims; call native vectors browser-WASM composition; or call deterministic sampling model quality. Any contract change requires a new incompatible profile and complete dependent invalidation."
sampling2_composition_preregistration_prohibition: "Do not generate Prompt2 comparative native or browser output before exact Tokenizer2, Template2, Sampling2, selected config, ordered stop IDs, SLM2 runtime/vocabulary, messages, rendered bytes, token IDs, context arithmetic, native/WASM interfaces, browser builds, profile schedule, zero-network policy, worker termination/recovery, resource bounds, output roots, verifier, and source-control parent are preregistered, committed, pushed, fetched, and actual-remote exact. Do not double-insert control tokens, reuse browser profiles, reuse a terminated worker, normalize or repair output, retry-select, hand-edit, overwrite, run the verifier more than once, or broaden a terminal failure into a skip or pass."
sampling2_binary_repository_prohibition: "Do not classify normative `.bin` authorities as text or allow autocrlf, clean filters, diff drivers, or merge drivers to change bytes. Require Git `text`, `diff`, and `merge` attributes unset, filtered and unfiltered blob identities equal, checked-in SHA256 equal the registered authority, and a regression test. The precommit filtered Q48 stage was rejected and must not be recreated or concealed."
historical_evidence_worktree_prohibition: "Do not validate immutable evidence by demanding that a mutable descendant checkout still equal a registered historical source closure. Resolve the exact canonical profile to its unique Git addition commit, require its sole parent to equal registrationParentCommit, and reopen registered files from that tree. Do not edit frozen profiles, verifiers, results, or raw evidence; weaken hostile or semantic tests; hide history drift; bypass absent package prerequisites; or convert a conditional missing-input boundary into a pass."
qwen3_template2_source_parity_prohibition: "Preserve completed registration commit `2693cec9eeff6af991c78d42a0eb9c9fe5716f60`, four private raw outputs, receipt SHA256 `d0a848a0827f536fb69b2d6f19ffb84102b1ff83ac2efed25e564fa9b2810cdb`, and public result SHA256 `c985f3c95e6554425de1743b723009cb92c17246eac4859c2f9feaa0aa5e3fa0`. Do not rerun any producer, observer, or verifier; edit, regenerate, narrow, reorder, replace, delete, relabel, normalize, escape, repair, truncate, hand-edit, select, reuse, or overwrite evidence; broaden the frozen supported branch; execute publisher Jinja in product runtime; add tools, thinking-enabled generation, role aliases, non-string content, legacy, migration, compatibility, repair, or fallback. Exact source/native parity is not browser-WASM, sampling, composition, model, quality, release, deployment, publication, MemoryEndpoints, or campaign evidence. Preregister every dependent browser/composition observation before output."
source_tree_cleanup_prohibition: "Do not recursively delete, move, empty, prune, or rewrite a path inside any source checkout because a path component is named `bin`, `release`, `packages`, `target`, `artifacts`, `output`, `cache`, `dist`, `build`, or another generic generated-content term. Do not use wildcard, substring, basename, or cross-shell cleanup rules as ownership proof. Before deletion, require an explicitly registered cleanup root, resolve and compare absolute paths in the same shell, prove the target is beneath that owned root and outside every source checkout, enumerate tracked membership, and abort if any candidate is tracked, unresolved, reparse-linked outside the root, or containment-ambiguous. If tracked source changes during a gate, fail closed, preserve the attempt and bounded incident evidence, restore only reviewed exact source-control blobs when justified, and rerun only the invalidated phase. Never invent process attribution or convert passing child output into a receipt after source-integrity failure."
qwen_tokenizer2_browser_wasm_preregistration_prohibition: "Do not generate full-artifact browser output before the exact preregistration checkpoint is committed, pushed, and fresh-remote exact. Do not substitute the Qwen model-config vocabulary size for the Tokenizer2 artifact header; the registered shape is 151,669 total, 151,643 base, and 26 added tokens. Do not edit profile, source closure, runner, verifier, module, adapter, corpus, native result, browser identity, limits, output names, or acceptance rules after observation begins. Do not create the output root early; run fewer or more than two independent profiles; reuse a profile; allow network; omit process closure or deletion; replace true worker termination with cooperative cancellation; repair recovery output; retry, select, normalize, reorder, truncate, hand-edit, or overwrite a run; invoke the verifier more than once; or turn terminal failure into a skip. Miniature WASM success and preregistration are not full-artifact browser parity, Template2, model runtime, model quality, release, deployment, publication, MemoryEndpoints, or campaign evidence."
qwen_tokenizer2_parity_v1_failure_v2_prohibition: "Do not call the terminal v1 verifier defect a Tokenizer2 mismatch, parity pass, parity rejection, or permission to edit producer output. Do not rerun verifier v1, create its missing receipt, reorder or normalize its Python JSON, replace any of its four raw files, reuse them, or delete the failed attempt. Do not modify v1 verifier/profile bytes to make the observed run pass. Preserve v1 as immutable failure evidence. Preserve successful v2 exactly: do not rerun either producer or verifier, replace or delete any result, alter object values or array order, repair or normalize bytes, select a retry, suppress history, overwrite the receipt, or broaden its native-only claim. Receipt SHA256 `4ebbfa2bc59d43bdbcd5b3b2528bed66035ff2a3165f554ac6867e7cc7fe0ea6` authorizes only a separately preregistered real-Chromium full-artifact WASM and resource gate."
qwen_tokenizer2_parity_preregistration_prohibition: "Preserve pushed preregistration commit `dc0f5f783de54b8c556064bc1d8a6b21d9cdb9a9`. Do not edit, regenerate, replace, narrow, reorder, or selectively execute its parity corpus, execution rules, verification rules, source closure, oracle, candidate runner, or verifier after comparative observation begins. Do not generate outputs before the preregistration commit is pushed and remote-exact; run fewer or more than the registered two oracle and two candidate executions; reuse an output path; normalize, repair, truncate, complete, retry-select, hand-edit, or discard raw output; choose favorable cases or seeds; change expected behavior after a mismatch; let either implementation under test rewrite the other's output; or call repository tests, a release-surface aggregate, process exit zero, deterministic repetition, or one matching case source-oracle parity. Preserve every mismatch and receiptless interrupted attempt. A 10-shard repository release-surface pass is not a product release, deployment, publication, browser-WASM pass, model-quality result, or campaign increment."
qwen_tokenizer2_conversion_claim_prohibition: "Do not call copied tokenizer JSON Tokenizer2, raw publisher Jinja Template2, structural conversion source-oracle parity, a native parser browser-WASM evidence, duplicate bytes model quality, or a conversion receipt product readiness. Do not admit unknown source fields, unsupported processors, redundant vocab or merge disagreement, source drift, non-create-only output, repaired tokens, or a legacy/fallback tokenizer path. Require the exact lock, closed parser, canonical artifact, independent reparse, duplicate deterministic conversion, preregistered source-oracle parity, and real browser-WASM evidence in dependency order."
tokenizer2_claim_and_legacy_prohibition: "Do not call the 7,459-byte canonical Tokenizer2 miniature a converted Qwen tokenizer, full source-oracle parity, production tokenizer, browser model, useful model, chat pass, release, deployment, publication, or campaign progress. Do not generate language-neutral authority bytes by invoking the Rust implementation under test; read tokenizer JSON, regex, or executable pipeline definitions at runtime; accept Unicode authority drift; dynamically compile artifact regex; silently normalize or replace invalid output; insert BOS/EOS automatically; permit unknown fields or flags; accept duplicate source archive members; add SLM1 or legacy tokenizer readers; support a version range, dual schema, alias, migration, compatibility shim, repair, or fallback; or begin dependent Template2 product integration before exact full pinned-Qwen conversion, receipt binding, broad tokenizers-0.22.2 parity, and native/browser-WASM full-artifact replay pass. Preserve miniature failures as contract evidence and keep full model/tokenizer bytes outside Git."
slm2_scalar_wasm_fixture_claim_prohibition: "Do not call canonical-fixture scalar/native, Node-WASM, or real-Chromium conformance a real-model runtime pass, source-framework parity, product browser activation, useful-model qualification, release, deployment, publication, or campaign progress. Do not use the embedded conformance fixture as the production model-transfer ABI, infer tokenizer or publisher-template behavior from token-ID vectors, infer stochastic sampling from greedy selection, or reuse the evidence after a bound format/runtime/vector identity changes. Require exact tokenizer/template vectors, declared seeded sampling, real converted artifact and source-framework parity, bounded production large-model ownership, untouched quality, functional acquisition, genuine multi-turn chat, and objective receipts."
cpu_pilot_trainer_authority_prohibition: "Do not call a wheelhouse manifest, environment lock, successful verifier, passing trainer test, checkpoint, adapter candidate, or candidate receipt training authorization or model quality. Do not modify the sealed environment or trainer without a new create-only versioned lock; install unowned packages into the captured environment; enable CUDA, remote code, remote model loading, network access, or child processes; truncate or pack sequences; change seeds after initialization; choose a best checkpoint; restart from incomplete evidence; overwrite or delete interrupted output; recover a terminally rejected run; repair outputs; or promote the unchanged rejected base. Only the current control program may issue a training lock after reopening exact source, curriculum, independent review, holdout, contamination, semantic, tombstone, environment, trainer, recipe, stop-rule, and output-root evidence. Environment lock v3 explicitly has `training_authority: false`."
holdout_custody_no_cheat_prohibition: "Do not author both development and independent-audit holdout contributions as one actor and disguise that fact with different hashes. Do not overwrite initialized templates, reuse an author identity or custody domain across splits, reorder or omit slots, reuse normalized prompts or template families, weaken factor diversity, expose raw prompts/anchors/seed, choose a seed after seeing materialized cases, assemble under a changed profile or control program, seal a factor pack without its exact custody receipt, substitute another same-length seed, edit factor-pack bytes after assembly, accept invalidated local initialization attempts, restore a direct sealer path, or call distinct hashes proof of organizational independence. Preserve incomplete and invalidated attempts as history. Empty templates, plans, controls, and tests are not authored holdouts, independent audit, sealing, curriculum acceptance, training authority, model quality, or campaign progress."
curriculum_review_evidence_prohibition: "Do not accept a curriculum row because it self-asserts reviewer_count, a decision hash, scan statuses, or a plausible summary. Do not bulk-approve, infer a missing reviewer, reuse one reviewer identity or custody domain, reuse one ledger file, omit or reorder records, review changed candidate/messages/response bytes, change a response during review, mark an always-required rubric item not applicable, accept a failed checklist or scan, adjudicate when primary reviewers agree, omit third-distinct adjudication when they disagree, retain a doubly rejected or adjudication-rejected record, forge a derived closure, place raw curriculum in the manifest or ledgers, store private review evidence in Git or outside C:/E:, trust a stale control program, or issue a training lock without reopening the exact manifest and every ledger. A manifest has no training authority, distinct hashes do not prove independent people, and tests of the review verifier are not real human review, curriculum acceptance, training, model quality, or campaign progress."
working_model_training_no_cheat_prohibition: "Do not train before an exact current profile, immutable source inventory, private sealed holdout receipt, hash-only exclusion index, accepted reviewed curriculum, complete public-fixture exclusions, lexical/entity/template/answer audit, independently bound semantic pass, tombstone lineage, environment lock, trainer identity, stop rules, and CPU pilot authorization all agree cryptographically. Do not place private prompts, expected anchors, seeds, teacher drafts, credentials, or model bytes in Git or public receipts. Do not copy evaluation prompts or expected answer terms into training; lie about template IDs or entities; omit failed reviews; bulk-approve; infer human review; include hidden reasoning; select an easy holdout seed; alter a case after output; repair teacher or model text; choose best-of-N records/checkpoints/outputs; relabel a failed adapter; retry without a new preregistration and material change; or promote unchanged rejected Qwen3 base bytes. Control-plane tests do not prove model quality, training authorization, conversion, runtime, browser, release, publication, or campaign progress."
working_model_structural_evidence_prohibition: "Do not promote the rejected Qwen2.5-0.5B base, its deterministic SLM2 conversion, or any structurally valid artifact from the converter foundation as a useful model, trained derivative, runtime pass, WASM pass, browser pass, release, deployment, publication, or campaign result. The base remains rejected at 2/8. Require the new fully pinned Qwen3-0.6B derivative, complete provenance, independent contamination controls, preregistered untouched behavioral pass, sole-SLM2 deterministic conversion, scalar-native inference, browser-WASM conformance, functional P2P-or-convert acquisition, and a genuine private multi-turn browser conversation before the vertical slice can complete."
qwen3_head_geometry_rewrite_prohibition: "Do not shoehorn Qwen3 query projections into the current head-count-times-head-dimension-equals-embedding-size invariant, truncate or reshape Q/K/V tensors, retain two SLM2 profiles, add format negotiation, preserve the superseded equality invariant through a compatibility reader, or call old Qwen3 conversion code authoritative. Replace the sole unpromoted SLM2 contract cleanly so query width is represented exactly, then regenerate and reverify the profile, vectors, independent oracle, hostile corpus, sanitizer seeds, converter, scalar runtime, and WASM consumers before using any prior evidence."
seeded_fuzz_evidence_prohibition: "Do not call a fuzz run seeded merely because a corpus-copy command was attempted. Require libFuzzer's terminal record to report the exact manifest-declared seed count, independently verify every source and external copied byte/hash before execution, retain the unmodified frozen copy separately from the mutable working corpus, bind exact tools and limits, and preserve zero-seed or otherwise defective runs as nonqualifying history. Do not infer model conversion, runtime safety, model quality, browser readiness, deployment, publication, or campaign progress from parser AddressSanitizer evidence."
slm2_legacy_and_claim_prohibition: "Do not add SLM1 support, legacy readers or writers, format aliases, version negotiation, migration shims, dual-format activation, permissive CBOR, hidden repair, best-effort parsing, parser disagreement tolerance, or fallback after an SLM2 rejection. Do not admit an arbitrary structurally valid tensor set: require the exact current canonical decoder tensor registry selected by model flags and layer count, and fail closed on missing, additional, renamed, reshaped, or wrongly indexed tensors. Do not weaken a frozen hostile case, choose only an easy fuzz seed, suppress a stable rejection code, reuse prior Node or fuzz evidence after a profile identity change, or call a compiled fuzz target a sustained sanitizer campaign. Do not claim that local vectors, fixtures, corpus replay, structural validation, distinct bytes, or one attractive output prove a real model, conversion readiness, browser readiness, quality, release, publication, or campaign progress. Require one current exact SLM2 profile, independent identity reproduction, bounded fail-closed parsing, sanitizer-backed fuzz evidence, real target integration, untouched raw outputs, and objective promotion receipts."
slm2_generator_assumption_prohibition: "Do not encode tensor string IDs, payload gaps, table offsets, or descriptor positions from one old vector as magic mutation constants. Derive hostile mutation targets from the current canonical header and descriptors, assert uniqueness and bounds, replay every declared verdict, and preserve any mismatch before correction. A new profile or vector invalidates dependent corpus and replay identities."
pre_release_no_debt_no_legacy_prohibition: Until the human explicitly removes this rule, do not incur technical debt or preserve obsolete behavior for compatibility. Do not add shims, dual contracts, migration branches, deprecated aliases, or fallback paths for superseded code. Rewrite cleanly, verify the replacement, remove superseded implementation, and retain immutable evidence only as audit truth rather than legacy support.
prelaunch_single_canonical_product_prohibition: "Until the human explicitly removes this rule, do not describe or implement TinyRustLM as an old supported product plus a V2 product. It is prelaunch and has one canonical current product architecture. Branch names, `vnext` paths, numeric schema generations, and exact names such as SLM2, Tokenizer2, Template2, Sampling2, and Prompt2 are technical source, wire, artifact, or evidence identities only; they do not authorize parallel runtime paths, backward compatibility, migration, version negotiation, aliases, fallback, retained deprecated code, or duplicated behavior. When a better design replaces prelaunch code, verify it end to end, update every dogfood consumer, and delete the superseded executable implementation in the same completed major change. Preserve immutable prior bytes and failures only as non-executable audit evidence."
pre_publication_no_debt_policy_id: pre-publication-clean-slate-v1
pre_publication_no_debt_prohibition: "Until the human explicitly removes this prohibition, do not add or retain technical debt, legacy or backward-compatibility support, compatibility shims, deprecated aliases, dual contracts, migration branches, fallback paths for superseded code, low-quality patches, knowingly temporary architecture, or deferred cleanup."
pre_publication_obsolete_contract_prohibition: "Do not preserve obsolete routes, schemas, behavior, or code merely because a dogfood client used them. Update every dogfood client to the verified current design and remove the obsolete path."
pre_publication_release_integrity_prohibition: "Do not deploy from a dirty, partially validated, or ambiguous release closure. A reachable dogfood or development surface is not evidence of a public release. Do not call work complete while known in-scope correctness, security, accessibility, test, documentation, or cleanup defects remain."
major_change_checkpoint_delay_prohibition: "Do not leave a coherent major change uncommitted or unpushed after its affected build and tests pass, and do not combine unrelated verified changes merely to postpone a checkpoint. Preserve each passing checkpoint promptly with exact evidence while continuing the larger unfinished campaign."
passing_release_delay_prohibition: "Do not leave an affected site undeployed after its complete applicable release gate passes and deployment authority is available. Do not call a partial unit suite, package creation, upload, local smoke, or unrelated green build 'all tests'; documentation, version history, secret scanning, clean source identity, authoritative transport verification, and public HTTPS readback remain mandatory parts of publication evidence."
model_acquisition_handoff_prohibition: "Do not make a user download a README, TXT file, shell script, PowerShell script, request JSON, conversion kit, or share kit to acquire or activate a model. Do not ask the user to find tools, edit paths, run conversion commands, or manually reunite model and composition artifacts. Do not fetch project-hosted model bytes, silently probe a desktop companion, accept mutable source revisions, use incomplete P2P identity evidence, load a model without its receipt-bound composition graph, or present conversion success before Rust validation and browser activation complete. Use one explicit-consent P2P-first state machine with companion-managed immutable conversion as its direct fallback."
composition_activation_prohibition: "Do not infer, synthesize, auto-select, or silently reuse an execution graph after model load. Do not generate before one exact bounded `.acg1` graph is activated under its expected lowercase SHA-256 identity. Do not accept missing, malformed, mismatched, changed, oversized, nonregular, or symlinked graph input, and do not count the deterministic package fixture as a product model or campaign result."
site_release_history_prohibition: "Do not publish changed site bytes without a new strictly increasing version and release entry. Do not reuse a version, invent or predeclare a successful publish time, overwrite or reorder prior release history, silently omit failed publication attempts from deployment evidence, or treat package creation, local validation, upload success, cache observation, or unit tests as successful public publication. Do not omit the visible version, release date, last-published value, Updates page, current release record, history record, exact secret scan, authoritative transport verification, or public HTTPS readback required by the current site release contract."
site_release_compatibility_prohibition: "Do not add readers, writers, aliases, migrations, dual schemas, or fallbacks for obsolete prerelease release-log formats. Update all dogfood publishers and consumers to one current verified contract and remove superseded implementations in the same completed change."
third_party_review_storage_prohibition: Do not place downloaded third-party repositories, packages, papers, standards, source archives, or model-review bytes inside a project repository or its source control. Store review copies only under `E:\Source\external\{project-name}`; commit only original project work and public-safe source identities, hashes, sizes, licenses, and bounded findings.
turbovec_turboquant_claim_prohibition: Do not conflate static model weights, online KV cache, and durable embedding indexes; do not claim TinyRustLM uses TurboVec or TurboQuant; and do not promote a dependency, format, bit width, one-bit-model label, speedup, memory reduction, recall, accuracy neutrality, or algorithm winner from report prose. Require pinned primary sources, scalar conformance, equal-byte comparisons, target-native and browser-WASM receipts, and untouched downstream quality evidence.
colibri_specialist_portfolio_claim_prohibition: Do not claim arbitrary layers, heads, tensors, vocabulary rows, or byte slices from a dense model are useful specialists; model-level routing is token-level MoE; disk streaming reduces total model bytes; physical deduplication reduces logical execution bytes; KV state is portable across model or adapter compositions; an atomic file pointer proves safe in-process switching; `attention_frozen` proves cache reuse; QLoRA requires per-token whole-layer requantization; hidden champion retry is acceptable routing; or TinyRustLM currently uses Colibri or a quality-proven specialist router. Require trained compatible subnetworks or adapters, immutable content identity without timestamps, direct non-destructive overlays, reference-counted atomic graph activation, full composition-change KV flush, honest byte categories, visible pre-generation fallback, sealed task and broad-quality receipts, and separate target-native/browser measurements.
authority:
  human_request: 2026-06-26
  route_elsewhere: Broad AI policy, harm essays, and alarm-led public messaging belong on other websites.
  human_update_2026_06_27: This website should not spend public copy on caution-centered or worry-centered AI messaging; keep that content on other sites.
  human_update_2026_06_27_site_rule: Runtime.MiRust.com pages should not be organized around warnings, cautions, worry, or alarm messaging; use proof, craft, lineage, local agency, and implementation-boundary language instead.
  human_update_2026_06_27_strategy_rule: Model-breeding, mutualist, tiny-ecology, and UAIX memory-strategy copy for this site should route away from warning/caution/worry messaging and toward proof, craft, lineage, fit, agency, and evidence.
  human_update_2026_06_27_no_meta_topic_rule: Do not make the absence of caution-centered messaging a public site topic; simply write in the proof, craft, lineage, fit, local-agency, and evidence voice.
  human_update_2026_06_27_external_topic_rule: Do not make Runtime.MiRust.com carry caution or worry-focused AI messaging; other websites can host that lane while this site stays on proof, craft, lineage, fit, local agency, and implementation evidence.
  human_update_2026_06_27_memory_strategy_rule: UAIX memory strategy and model-breeding reference copy for this site should stay in proof, craft, lineage, fit, local agency, and implementation evidence voice.
  human_update_2026_06_27_direct_request_rule: For this website, Model Breeding and UAIX File Handoff strategy should be framed as craft, route, lineage, evidence, fit, and local agency; broader concern-led AI copy belongs elsewhere.
  human_update_2026_06_28_site_scope_rule: Keep Runtime.MiRust.com public copy out of warnings/cautions/worry framing; those topics belong on other websites while this site carries proof, craft, lineage, fit, local agency, and implementation evidence.
  human_update_2026_06_28_direct_site_scope_rule: For this website, UAIX strategy and Model Breeding copy should stay centered on routes, lineage, proof, and local implementation evidence; concern-led messaging belongs on separate sites.
  human_update_2026_06_28_setup_file_handoff_rule: UAIX setup-file-handoff and Model Breeding memory copy should appear as route, lineage, proof, fit, local agency, and implementation evidence for this site.
  human_update_2026_06_28_latest_direct_rule: Runtime.MiRust.com should not talk about or organize around cautions; other websites can carry that topic while this site carries proof, craft, lineage, fit, local agency, routes, and implementation evidence.
  human_update_2026_06_28_model_breeding_memory_rule: Model Breeding concepts, research references, UAIX File Handoff strategy, short-term routes, and long-term pointer memory should keep the site voice centered on route maps, candidate lineage, evidence, and local fit.
  human_update_2026_06_28_setup_handoff_site_rule: UAIX setup-file-handoff strategy should be used here as constructive routing memory for reports, research, short-term routes, and long-term checksum pointers; caution-centered commentary belongs on separate sites.
  human_update_2026_06_28_latest_direct_rule_2: Keep warnings, cautions, worry, and concern-led messaging out of Runtime.MiRust.com public copy; this file is the intentional local place to name those taboo terms while the site speaks in routes, lineage, proof, fit, local agency, and implementation evidence.
  human_update_2026_06_28_source_backed_route_rule: Model Breeding, UAIX File Handoff, and source-backed ALR1 route copy for this site should stay in route, lineage, proof, fit, local agency, and implementation evidence language; warning, caution, and worry language belongs only in this taboo routing file when it must be named.
  human_update_2026_06_28_modelbreeder_research_rule: The 2026-06-28 ModelBreeder research intake should guide Runtime.MiRust.com toward fitness, scope notes, breeding invariants, evaluation checkpoints, local agency, proof-of-use, genome lineage, and implementation fit language.
  human_update_2026_06_29_latest_direct_rule: Keep this website focused on Model Breeding routes, research references, proof, lineage, fit, local agency, and implementation evidence; caution-centered wording belongs here only as taboo routing memory.
  human_update_2026_06_29_federated_strategy_rule: Federated/local-update and UAIX File Handoff strategy copy for this site should stay centered on candidate routes, proof-of-use, checksum pointers, lineage, local fit, and implementation evidence.
  human_update_2026_07_14_browser_first_local_app_consent_rule: TinyRustLM is designed to work 100 percent in the web browser. Desktop companion features are totally optional. The browser must never unexpectedly probe, open, pair with, launch, or request access to a program on the user's computer. A user must first choose a clearly labeled desktop feature, be told what local program will be contacted and why, and be told to expect the browser or operating-system access prompt before any local connection attempt occurs.
  human_update_2026_07_14_local_and_worldwide_memory_choice_rule: Local copied memory and local queued synchronization must remain available choices. MemoryEndpoints.com should be encouraged as the optional way to keep memory available worldwide and across devices, but it must never be presented as mandatory or as available without internet.
  human_update_2026_07_15_pre_release_no_debt_rule: TinyRustLM is not a live released product. Until the human explicitly removes this rule, no technical debt, legacy support, or backward-compatibility work is allowed; clean replacement and full rewrites are authorized whenever they produce the best architecture.
  human_update_2026_07_15_final_attestation_integrity_rule: Pre-release or locally generated evidence must never be promoted through a self-asserted signature Boolean. Final completion requires the exact content-addressed campaign predicate, all objective gates, no open release-blocking risk, and cryptographic authorization from an approved release authority.
pre_release_engineering_taboo:
  standing_rule: Remains active until the human explicitly removes it.
  product_status: Publicly reachable development or test surfaces do not make TinyRustLM a live released product.
  prohibited_work:
    - Do not preserve superseded behavior merely for compatibility.
    - Do not add compatibility shims, dual schemas, migration branches, deprecated aliases, legacy fallbacks, or version bridges for unreleased behavior.
    - Do not knowingly defer cleanup, tests, architecture repair, or contract correction as technical debt.
    - Do not describe pre-release evidence, fixtures, local execution, or reachable development surfaces as a live release.
  required_replacement_behavior:
    - Prefer the cleanest architecture even when that requires rewriting an existing implementation.
    - Remove superseded implementation, tests, and documentation after the replacement is verified.
    - Keep immutable receipts, rejected attempts, and failure history; audit evidence is historical truth, not supported legacy behavior.
    - Record genuine external blockers without converting them into skips, simulations, or compatibility exceptions.
campaign_completion_taboo:
  current_truth: The campaign final attestation is content-addressed but remains unsigned, unauthorized, incomplete, and non-promotable.
  prohibited_shortcuts:
    - Do not treat successful final-report generation as campaign completion.
    - Do not accept `signatureVerified`, `authorizedRelease`, `passed`, or another Boolean without the strict current verification receipt and its exact cryptographic parents.
    - Do not generate, guess, request in plaintext, persist, or log a private signing key.
    - Do not sign an incomplete predicate, an old source revision, a stale receipt set, or a campaign with an open release-blocking risk.
    - Do not let local fixtures, QEMU, loopback reachability, package creation, or public development URLs satisfy live-service, physical-hardware, outside-network, publication, or release-authorization gates.
    - Do not claim MemoryEndpoints individual-account setup or continuity without an advertised production contract and authorized live receipts for those exact accounts.
    - Do not place dashboard output, secret-scan output, release-authorization verification, or final-attestation output inside the prerequisite public secret-scan identity; that creates an impossible self-referential hash cycle.
  required_authorization_path:
    - Generate the unsigned predicate only from a rebuilt healthy receipt DAG and dashboard v8.
    - Sign the canonical authorization envelope with an active Ed25519 `release-authorizer` key from the strict source-bound authority registry.
    - Verify registry, key, signature, predicate, source revision, issue time, expiry, and envelope identity before creating deployment evidence.
    - Require that exact unexpired verification receipt as the final-attestation parent and preserve every earlier incomplete or superseded receipt.
    - Keep the public evidence graph acyclic: scan ordinary publishable artifacts first, then validate scope-derived dashboard, scan, authorization, and attestation artifacts through their strict dedicated schemas, hashes, signatures, immutable history, and exact readback.
browser_first_and_local_app_consent:
  product_default: TinyRustLM must provide its core AI chat experience entirely in the web browser without requiring a desktop application.
  optional_desktop_boundary:
    - Desktop companion features are optional enhancements for users who deliberately choose local P2P hosting, companion-managed model storage, service operation, or another clearly documented desktop capability.
    - Browser-only users must not be pressured into installing, pairing, or granting access to a desktop program.
    - The absence of a desktop app must be a normal supported state, not an error or degraded-trust state.
  prohibited_unexpected_behavior:
    - Do not probe loopback ports, custom protocols, native messaging hosts, installed applications, browser extensions, local services, or desktop companions before explicit user initiation.
    - Do not launch or attempt to launch a local program automatically.
    - Do not trigger a browser, firewall, antivirus, operating-system, or application access prompt before the product has explained that the prompt is expected.
    - Do not hide local-app access inside page load, model search, chat send, memory setup, P2P discovery, advanced-panel expansion, background retry, or capability detection.
    - Do not treat prior installation, a remembered setting, or detection of a local endpoint as fresh consent for a new browser session unless the user explicitly enabled a narrowly scoped reconnect behavior and can revoke it.
  required_opt_in_flow:
    - Present a clear user action whose label names the optional desktop capability, such as Connect desktop companion or Enable local P2P hosting.
    - Before any connection attempt, explain which local application will be contacted, what capability it provides, what data is exchanged, and whether the browser or operating system may show an access prompt.
    - Require an affirmative user action after that explanation.
    - Attempt only the explained, narrowly scoped operation after consent.
    - If the app is absent, unavailable, or denied, return quietly to the fully usable browser-only path with an honest status and no repeated background prompts.
    - Provide a visible disconnect or disable control and honor it immediately.
  trust_and_release_boundary:
    - An unexpected attempt to access a program on the user's computer is a release-blocking trust failure.
    - Any browser or operating-system local-access prompt that appears without prior in-product explanation is a release-blocking defect.
    - Repeated probing after denial, cancellation, disconnect, or browser-only selection is prohibited.
    - This boundary exists because surprising local access will reasonably feel invasive, damage TinyRustLM's reputation, and make users unwilling to trust or use the application.
  required_tests:
    - A fresh browser-only visit performs no loopback, custom-protocol, native-messaging, extension, or installed-app probe.
    - Ordinary chat, local browser model use, model search, and optional MemoryEndpoints use perform no desktop access attempt.
    - The optional desktop flow displays the pre-access explanation before the first local request or system prompt.
    - Cancel, deny, disconnect, and browser-only choices prevent all background retries.
    - Network evidence proves that local-app traffic begins only after the recorded user opt-in event.
    - Browser-only behavior remains complete when no companion is installed.
memory_location_and_offline_choice:
  required_local_options:
    - Users may keep an inspected local UAIX Memory Deck as an offline memory context.
    - Users may keep locally queued synchronization changes for later delivery without falsely treating that queue as readable offline long-term memory.
    - Local copied memory and local synchronization choices must remain available even when the user does not enroll in MemoryEndpoints.com.
  worldwide_option:
    - Encourage MemoryEndpoints.com for memory that should remain available worldwide, across browsers, and across devices.
    - Describe MemoryEndpoints.com as optional hosted synchronization, not as a requirement for local chat or local memory.
    - State that new hosted reads, writes, and synchronization require internet access.
  contextual_offline_explanation:
    - Explain offline readiness from the user's current model and memory state instead of making a blanket promise.
    - A loaded browser model may continue chatting offline in the current open tab while it remains loaded.
    - If hosted memory is active, tell the user to load a local UAIX Memory Deck before disconnecting when offline memory context is needed.
    - Locally queued sync changes may upload after reconnection, but they must not be described as a complete offline memory copy.
public_site_voice:
  preferred_emphasis:
    - browser-local Rust/WASM execution
    - transparent .slm artifacts and manifests
    - model breeding as traceable lineage, compatibility, candidate evaluation, and craft
    - tiny model ecology, adapters, quantization, and local experiments
    - warm research language around growth, fit, interoperability, and proof
  taboo_modes:
    - alarm-led AI essays
    - speculative doom framing
    - public copy centered on fear, panic, or worry
    - public copy organized around cautions instead of craft, lineage, proof, and local agency
    - broad governance sermons when the page is about TinyRustLM capability
    - memory-strategy copy that makes concern management the subject instead of routes, evidence, and project fit
  taboo_terms_for_public_copy:
    - warning
    - warnings
    - caution
    - cautions
    - worry
    - fear
    - panic
  replacement_style:
    warning: use proof, boundary, status, route, or fit when the copy needs a factual project edge.
    caution: use scope note, implementation boundary, or future lane when the copy needs project routing.
    risk: use fit, compatibility, or claim boundary when the copy needs technical precision.
    site_section_titles: use status, evidence, boundaries, routes, fit, lineage, craft, or future lanes.
implementation_note: This file guides site voice and public product copy; technical tests, error states, and Rust lint names may still use their exact domain terms where required.
